hes and split-levels, new subdivisions and military rentals</dd></div></dl></div><div><p class="local-k">Local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Severe thunderstorms</li><li>Tornadoes</li><li>Hail</li><li>Hurricane remnants</li><li>Ice storms</li></ul></div></div>

<h2>Central Delaware storm exposure</h2>
<p>Dover sits in the flat middle of the state, exposed to storms from every direction. Summer squall lines, the occasional hail core and tropical remnants all cross Kent County. Tropical Storm Isaias in 2020 produced a long-track tornado that crossed Kent and New Castle counties, a reminder that tornado damage isn’t just a Midwestern problem.</p>
<h2>A wide range of housing</h2>
<p>Insurance appraisal in Dover covers a lot of ground. Federal-era and Victorian homes near The Green and Legislative Hall carry historic materials and, in some cases, design-review requirements. Mid-century ranches in areas like Rodney Village and Kent Acres have older roofs and original plumbing. Newer subdivisions toward Camden and Magnolia bring vinyl siding, architectural shingles and HOA rules about matching.</p>
<h2>Military families and rentals</h2>
<p>With Dover Air Force Base nearby, many homes are rented or owned by families who may be reassigned mid-claim. We work by phone, email and shared documents so a claim can move forward even if you’ve relocated, and we coordinate inspections with tenants or property managers.</p>
<h2>What we see most</h2>
<ul>
<li>Wind-damaged roofs and siding after summer storms</li>
<li>Hail on roofs, gutters and HVAC units</li>
<li>Tree and limb damage in older, shaded neighborhoods</li>
<li>Burst pipes during cold snaps</li>
<li>Commercial roof claims along Route 13</li>
</ul>

